# ---------------------------------------------------------------- build ----
# Rebuilt from theatres.sqlite3 by ./export.py on every run, so committing it would
# only create merge noise. The daily workflow builds it fresh and uploads it as
# a Pages artifact.
dist/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- python ----
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
env/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ node ----
# web/package.json exists only so `node --test` reads the .js files there as ES
# modules. There are no dependencies, but nothing should stop an accidental
# `npm install` from being committed.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
npm-debug.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- sqlite ----
# Write-ahead-log sidecars: transient, recreated on open, and committing them
# would put a half-written transaction in git. Checkpoint before committing the
# database (`PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)`) so the .sqlite3 file is complete
# on its own.
*.sqlite3-wal
*.sqlite3-shm
*.sqlite3-journal

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- os -----
# .DS_Store leaks directory listings and window positions; the AppleDouble
# files leak resource forks.
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# -------------------------------------------------------------- editors ----
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# --------------------------------------------------------------- secrets ----
# Nothing here needs a credential — GitHub Pages publishes with the built-in
# GITHUB_TOKEN — but this repo is public, so make an accidental one hard.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
secrets.json

# ------------------------------------------------------------ local notes ----
# The working note for whoever picks this up next: which machine account pushes,
# which SSH alias bypasses the personal default, what is left to do. All of it is
# specific to one laptop and stale the moment it is published, and it names a
# personal address. It stays on disk and out of the repo; README.md,
# ARCHITECTURE.md and REQUIREMENTS.md carry everything a reader actually needs.
HANDOFF.md

# ============================================================================
# Deliberately NOT ignored — these carry the project's history and must ship:
#
#   theatres.sqlite3   Every soft-delete, venue_changes row, first-seen date and
#                  geocode. A run starting from an empty database would lose
#                  that history AND re-geocode all 476 venues daily, which
#                  would get the project blocked by Nominatim within a week.
#   snapshots/     Raw wikitext of every revision fetched, so a bad upstream
#                  edit can always be diffed against what came before.
#   web/data/venues.json
#                  Written by export.py, so it looks like build output — but
#                  test_bundle.py reads it, and the workflow runs the tests
#                  before export.py. Ignoring it breaks the first gate on a
#                  fresh checkout.
#   web/CNAME      The custom domain. GitHub Pages reads it from inside the
#                  published artifact and drops the domain on any deploy that
#                  omits it.
#   web/admin.*    Local-only tooling. Kept in the repo but never published:
#                  export.py builds dist/ from an allow-list and then re-scans
#                  the output, refusing if anything admin-shaped got in.
# ============================================================================
